  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>correct approach</b> in this scenario is to log into the AWS Management Console as the root user, delete the incorrect bucket policy, and reinstate the previously functional policy. The <b>root user</b> has unrestricted access to all AWS services and resources in the account, even if a new bucket policy denies access to IAM users. <br><br><b>Tips:</b> If an S3 bucket policy locks out all other IAM users (sometimes even administrators, due to a deny statement), the root user is the only identity guaranteed full access and can remedy policy mistakes.<br><br><i>\"Execute the AWS CLI command 'aws s3api put-bucket-policy' using an IAM user\"</i> is incorrect because if the newly applied policy denies access, even privileged IAM users may be unable to make changes.<br><br><i>\"Any IAM user from the AWS account can use the AWS Console...\"</i> is not correct: explicit deny statements in a bucket policy override permissions that an IAM user might otherwise have.<br><br><i>\"Reach out to AWS Support to help you retrieve and reinstate the lost S3 bucket policy\"</i> is not necessary in most cases, as the account root user can always recover access themselves without AWS support intervention.<br><br><b>Summary:</b> Use the root account in the AWS console to fix S3 bucket policy mistakes that have locked out all other users.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your DevOps team uses an S3 bucket named <strong>artifacts-bucket</strong> for storing critical files produced by your CI/CD pipelines.<br>An intern mistakenly deleted the bucket's policy and applied a new (potentially incorrect) policy. What is the most appropriate way to restore the original working bucket policy?</p>",
